  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe slow moving Nor'Easter that has been impacting the region\nover the last few days is now located just south of eastern Long\nIsland, based on the latest visible satellite imagery. Radar\nimagery shows bands of light rain continues to rotate east to\nwest across the region on the northwest side of the system.\nRainfall rates have been fairly light with this activity, with\nhourly rates mainly under a tenth of an inch per hour. CAMs and\nother short-term model guidance suggest these bands of rainfall\nwill continue through the evening hours, with activity winding\ndown from west to east tonight. All areas should be done with\nprecipitation by about midnight to 2 AM or so, although plenty \nof low clouds and mist will continue into the late night hours.\nThe storm center is expected to weaken as it slowly drifts\nnorthward across eastern New England and will be getting close\nto the Gulf of Maine and Nova Scotia by Tuesday morning. \n\nAdditional rainfall this afternoon into this evening will\ngenerally be around a tenth of an inch across eastern New York,\nalthough localized totals around a quarter to half inch are\npossible across the high terrain of western New England. Despite\nthis rainfall, no flooding or hydro issues are expected. \n\nWith the storm weakening, winds have decreased across the region\nas well. Northerly winds are still in the 5-10 mph range and\nthese will continue to decrease, with northwesterly winds of 5\nmph or less for the overnight hours. With the weakening storm\nsystem and winds dying down, tidal flooding is no longer\nexpected to be a concern on the Hudson River in the mid Hudson\nValley. While river levels are still near action stage during\nhigh tide this afternoon and again overnight, it is no longer\nexpected to cause flooding. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nBehind the departing storm system, upper level heights will be\nrising, beginning on Tuesday. South to southwest flow aloft \nwill allow for warming temps aloft through the late week. Dry\nweather is expected on Tuesday, although there will continue to\nbe a decent amount of clouds around through the day on Tuesday.\nWith enough low level moisture around, some patchy fog is\nexpected to develop on Tuesday night, especially in valley\nareas. \n\nIt be continued dry for Wednesday into Thursday with high\npressure just south of the area. Highs will be turning above\nnormal, with valley areas reaching into the 70s on both\nWednesday and Thursday. Overnight lows will be mild enough to\nkeep frost away, with lows in the 40s and 50s on Tuesday night\nand mainly in the 50s for Wednesday night. \n\nThe next chance for rain will be some passing showers ahead of a\ncold front on Friday. Temperatures will continue to be very mild\non Friday, with highs well into the 70s once again. Although\ntemps will be slightly lower over the weekend, it will continue\nto remain mild. Another chance for more widespread rain is\nexpected over the weekend (mainly on Sunday), although the exact\ntiming and track of this is still uncertain this far out.",
